{"qid":"Q1472004","names":["Yan"],"title":"Yan","endonym":null,"capsule":"Yan was one of the seven major Warring States of ancient China, centered on the lower Liao River and Hai River basins in the area of present-day Beijing and Hebei province, with its capital at Ji (modern Beijing). The state traced its founding to the early Zhou period (c. 11th century BCE), and survived as a peripheral northern power until the Warring States era, when it reached its apex under King Zhao of Yan (r. 311–279 BCE), who rebuilt the state after a near-fatal succession crisis and launched a famous multi-state assault on Qi (284 BCE) that captured seventy cities. Yan's final king, Yan Xi, fled to Liaodong after the Qin general Wang Jian captured Ji in 226 BCE; Qin completed the conquest in 222 BCE, absorbing Yan into the unified empire.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The core territory around Ji is well attested; outer boundaries fluctuated substantially across the Warring States period and are reconstructed from textual sources with only approximate cartographic precision.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Yan (state)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yan_(state)"},{"label":"Britannica: Warring States period","url":"https://www.britannica.com/event/Warring-States-period"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Yan (state)","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Marquis Hui of Yan","nativeName":"燕惠侯","roman":"Yān Huì Hóu","start":-864,"end":-827,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Marquis Xi of Yan","nativeName":"燕釐侯","roman":"Yān Xí Hóu","start":-826,"end":-791,"image":